<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🍂 LIVE LEAF COLOR</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE LEAF COLOR</h2><p><b>Gradient key</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/leaf_color/legend.png" width="600" alt="gradient key"><br>What each color means (one trip around the year, left to right on the key): <b>deep blue</b> = dormant winter; <b>light blue/cyan</b> = spring awakening and bud break; <b>teal-green</b> = leaf emergence; <b>green</b> = growing season; <b>yellow-green/yellow</b> = first color; <b>orange</b> = autumn coloring; <b>red/magenta</b> = peak color into leaf drop; <b>purple</b> = late drop returning to <b>deep-blue</b> dormant (the scale wraps: both ends are winter). Color is the satellite-derived growth stage, not the color of individual trees. Only land the satellite actually saw this composite is painted — <b>transparent = clouds, snow, cities, bare ground, water, or otherwise no current observation</b>.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NASA MODIS Aqua MYD13A1/MYD09A1 via Planetary Computer (global, 500 m)).<br/><b>Data time:</b> VI composite start 2026-08-05 (+16d window); reflectance 2026-08-13 (+8d); retrieved 2026-09-26 06:24 AM EDT<br/><b>Source version:</b> leaf-v7-1ce56c63a4cc<br/><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE COMPOSITE</p><p>Turn on/off independently of all other layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <NetworkLink>
      <name>🍂 LIVE LEAF COLOR — auto-refresh</name>
      <Link>
        <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/kml/live/Great_Lakes_Live_Leaf_Color.kml</href>
        <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
        <refreshInterval>21600</refreshInterval>
      </Link>
    </NetworkLink>
  </Document>
</kml>
